    questionText: "What is the value of document A as a historical source on Elizabethan Dublin? Make reference to the document in your answer.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal"}, totalMarks: 10,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "The ‘Civic Regulations for the Protection of the City’, 1558-1600, are a primary source providing evidence about conditions in the city during the Nine Years’ War, a key period in Irish history.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-2", kind: "point", verbatim: "The ‘Civic Regulations’ give us information on how the city was governed and on official attempts to cope with rebellion/war in Ireland.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-3", kind: "point", verbatim: "The year-by-year development in the regulations, such as the doubling of the number guarding the suburbs in 1599, show the increasing impact of the war on Dublin.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-4", kind: "point", verbatim: "The official attitude to “Irishmen” is shown in the 1600 Regulations. “All Irishmen” entering the city are to be noted, and any “suspicious character” is to be questioned by the Mayor. This shows the divisions between the city officials who are loyal to England and the native Irish who are not trusted.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-5", kind: "point", verbatim: "References to money show how the city authorities wanted to save themselves any expenses, with the cost of guarding the city to be “borne by the citizens” and “strange beggars” to be forbidden access to the city, etc.", marks: null },
    ],
