    id: "hist-2025-hl-lm-s1-q2a", topicId: "hist-lm-eur-3", conceptId: "extent-question-value-marching-london",
    section: "1", questionRef: "2025 HL Later Modern Section 1 Q2(a)",
    stem: "Case study: The Jarrow March, October 1936",
    questionText: "To what extent do both documents question the value of marching on London? Explain your answer, referring to both documents.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal"}, totalMarks: 10,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "Both documents question the value of marching on London, document A more so than document B. Points could include:", marks: null },
    { id: "r-2", kind: "point", verbatim: "In doc A, the government view of marches on London is entirely negative. The statement says that such marches “can do no good”, are “liable to cause hardships” and are “altogether undesirable”.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-3", kind: "point", verbatim: "The government states that the parliamentary system, where each voter has a member of parliament to represent him/her, is the only way to influence government policy, and that marches have no “constitutional role”.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-4", kind: "point", verbatim: "The government sees marches as futile as no government minister will “consent” to meet any group of marchers.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-5", kind: "point", verbatim: "Doc B also sees the shortcomings of marches, “these weary marches certainly are useless”. Doc B suggests that the purpose of some marches may be “propagandist” or to “stir up discontent”.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-6", kind: "point", verbatim: "However, doc B also acknowledges the genuine grievances of the Jarrow marchers, “It is not to be wondered at that these men… should feel they have been neglected”. Marchers have been out of work for many months and whole towns have had “all their industries disappear”.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-7", kind: "point", verbatim: "Doc B criticises parliament as often “forgetful, unimaginative and lazy”, and suggests that governments need to be prodded into action by “popular pressure”.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-8", kind: "point", verbatim: "The writer praises the marchers for having drawn attention to the “desperate conditions” in Jarrow and sees the marchers not as troublemakers but as “men who are anxious that they shall not be forgotten”.", marks: null },
    ],
