    stem: "Case study: The divorce of Henry VIII and Catherine of Aragon",
    questionText: "To what extent do both documents confirm the importance of the monarch at that time? Explain your answer, referring to both documents.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal"}, totalMarks: 10,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "The importance of the monarch at that time could be reflected in points such as:", marks: null },
    { id: "r-2", kind: "point", verbatim: "Marriages between European royal families are negotiated by diplomats who check the suitability and status of the prospective partners. These marriages are of high political and international importance.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-3", kind: "point", verbatim: "Henry VIII has come to believe that God is indignant that he married his brother’s wife and therefore punished him by sending no male heirs. This reflects Henry’s inflated sense of his own importance.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-4", kind: "point", verbatim: "Henry believes that the welfare of the kingdom is directly threatened by his lack of a male heir. He fears instability and danger if he does not have a son to succeed him. The welfare of the kingdom depends on the welfare of the king.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-5", kind: "point", verbatim: "The fact that Cavendish did not write his account of the divorce until after Henry’s death suggests that the writer was too afraid of the king to write about the divorce during Henry’s life time. This shows the power and importance of Henry VIII, who dealt with his opponents by executing them.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-6", kind: "point", verbatim: "Cavendish illustrates the importance of the monarchy by describing what he sees as the negative impact of Henry VIII on England.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-7", kind: "point", verbatim: "Henry VIII’s “foolish” love life has brought ruination to the kingdom in many ways. Monasteries have been closed down, depriving the poor from the relief they used to provide. Religious conflicts and executions have caused the deaths of many people.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-8", kind: "point", verbatim: "The “will and wilful desire” of the king has brought the noble kingdom almost to destruction.", marks: null },
    { id: "r-9", kind: "point", verbatim: "The impact of a king’s actions far outlasts him, showing the king’s importance in society. The “fatal love” for Anne Boleyn was soon over but “the plague” caused by the king’s determination to divorce Catherine and marry Anne “has not yet ceased” even after several decades.", marks: null },
    ],
